{"title":"2026 AMC 8 Regional Problems/Problem 18","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem_18\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 18</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#See_Also_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_18\">Problem 18</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AMC_8_Regional_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 18\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>As shown in the figure below, a hexagon is formed by nine equilateral triangles of five different sizes. Given that the side length of the largest equilateral triangle among them is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/9/e290f2cba0ba0e11c0b91630da15a408da2fcf1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$24$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> centimeters, what is the perimeter of this hexagon in centimeters?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/2/772c17d38a83713b6aabb90065f4c0677e8fb555.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 112\\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ 116\\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ 120\\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ 124\\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ 128$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"442\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AMC_8_Regional_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let the side lengths of the five different sizes of equilateral triangles be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/d/52d9871f0ebe04faa75b50d7055c8a05d08120c2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a, b, c, d,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"66\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/a/a3a59bb1293ee3f6dec19de4019a7178874219ae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> in increasing order, where the largest side length is given as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/7/847ff0dab8be8a77777a85bbb6fecc919382e555.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e = 24$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"53\" height=\"14\" />. By examining how the sides of the adjacent triangles touch along the edges inside the hexagon, we can set up a system of linear equations to determine the relative sizes of all the smaller triangles. From the geometric layout of the interlocking shapes, tracking the side boundaries reveals that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/0/520f94cdb91d89ed6da825c35dd4ff5a2c82bd44.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d = 16$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"54\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/e/2eeb57269daee143ec45dcedeadf422f2cf9b271.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c = 12$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"49\" height=\"12\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/2/a7203b7e6f070ba402b9cd45dee2bfc125164fd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b = 10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"52\" height=\"14\" />, and the smallest triangle has a side length of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f98b3f405113241659d8c8db2ea3480346db4d0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a = 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"14\" />. The outer perimeter of the combined hexagon is composed of specific exposed side segments from these triangles. Summing up these external boundary edges around the entire figure yields a total perimeter of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/e/0aed56990dedba5a88a65e21245ccfd5d5e43ae2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$128$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> centimeters.\n</p><p>The correct answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/e/e8e4413146130f67e20c4207dbba7d051910e3d3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(E)}\\ 128}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"73\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~Icosahedron0288\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AMC_8_Regional_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AMC_8_Regional_Problems\" title=\"2026 AMC 8 Regional Problems\">2026 AMC 8 Regional Problems</a></li></ul>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AMC_8_Regional_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let the side lengths of the five different sizes of equilateral triangles be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/d/52d9871f0ebe04faa75b50d7055c8a05d08120c2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a, b, c, d,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"66\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/a/a3a59bb1293ee3f6dec19de4019a7178874219ae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> in increasing order, where the largest side length is given as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/7/847ff0dab8be8a77777a85bbb6fecc919382e555.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e = 24$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"53\" height=\"14\" />. By examining how the sides of the adjacent triangles touch along the edges inside the hexagon, we can set up a system of linear equations to determine the relative sizes of all the smaller triangles. From the geometric layout of the interlocking shapes, tracking the side boundaries reveals that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/0/520f94cdb91d89ed6da825c35dd4ff5a2c82bd44.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d = 16$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"54\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/e/2eeb57269daee143ec45dcedeadf422f2cf9b271.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c = 12$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"49\" height=\"12\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/2/a7203b7e6f070ba402b9cd45dee2bfc125164fd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b = 10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"52\" height=\"14\" />, and the smallest triangle has a side length of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f98b3f405113241659d8c8db2ea3480346db4d0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a = 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"14\" />. The outer perimeter of the combined hexagon is composed of specific exposed side segments from these triangles. Summing up these external boundary edges around the entire figure yields a total perimeter of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/e/0aed56990dedba5a88a65e21245ccfd5d5e43ae2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$128$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> centimeters.\n</p><p>The correct answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/e/e8e4413146130f67e20c4207dbba7d051910e3d3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(E)}\\ 128}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"73\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~viviviiccotrrr1098\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also_2\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AMC_8_Regional_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AMC_8_Regional_Problems\" title=\"2026 AMC 8 Regional Problems\">2026 AMC 8 Regional Problems</a></li></ul>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017132253\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.012 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.023 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 116/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 3648/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:37401:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017132253 and revision id 291900. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}